WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.171 --> 00:00:03.180
(African violin music)

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.560
- [Sudan] There's like a strong

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.773
violin culture in Africa as a whole.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.730
Being able to see the violin culture

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.350
in all of these different ways.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:15.020
It just resonated with me
because I am African-American,

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.120
and I know that my African
roots are from somewhere.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.790
It just opened up my whole mind

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.173
to the idea of violin, and
like what you can do with it.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.770
("Come Meh Way" plays)

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.280
My name is Sudan Archives,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.170
and I am a singer, song writer, producer,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.073
and violinist from Cincinnati, Ohio.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:41.770
My songs are all about textures.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.350
It's just one idea with all of these ways

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:45.550
to get different sounds.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.604
My songs usually start with my violin.

00:00:48.604 --> 00:00:51.950
And then once I find like
a idea on the violin,

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.630
I usually have like my
loop station with me.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.170
I'm able to just add more
layers and more layers

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.670
and more layers to that.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.453
I can pitch the octave down.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.730
I can just make it sound like an accordion

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:05.983
or wind instruments.

00:01:06.902 --> 00:01:09.600
Being able to have technology is probably

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.510
why I'm able to like be a solo artist,

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:13.261
because I wouldn't be able to do

00:01:13.261 --> 00:01:15.641
all of these things at once.

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:18.974
(intense violin ending)

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.109
This is my song "Nont For
Sale" off my EP "Sink"

00:01:27.109 --> 00:01:30.809
♪ This is my light, don't block the sun ♪

00:01:30.809 --> 00:01:34.690
♪ This is my seat, can't you tell ♪

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.423
To start a song I usually
start with violin.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.810
The whole format of "Nont For Sale"

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.657
is just this simple violin riff.

00:01:44.657 --> 00:01:47.430
(violin riff plays)

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.040
The style of the plucking

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.160
came from like my fiddle background.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.320
All the other melodic parts

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.233
are basically variations of that riff.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:07.367
My parents raised us in like
this strict church environment.

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:10.200
("Wake Up" plays)

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.170
My biological dad is a preacher

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.170
like we had to read bible verses
and write pages about them.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.360
My step dad, he was a music producer.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.603
He really wanted me to pursue music.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.363
He just really pushed like
the creative side of me.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.607
Baroque Violin Shop is
a store in Cincinnati.

00:02:35.607 --> 00:02:36.440
Hi!

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:38.703
- Good to see you again.

00:02:38.703 --> 00:02:40.759
- Good to see you too!

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:41.730
- What do we got?

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.190
- I first got into violin
because the owner at

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.680
Baroque Violin Shop came to my school

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.330
and played the cello, played the violin,

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.090
and had like this really
cool story with it.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.713
But also like using loop
stations and effect pedals.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.680
That was when I was like,
OK, I need to get a violin.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.763
(Sudan plays violin)

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.160
I'd still be making donuts
and waitressing or something

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.510
or in school maybe,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:17.380
if I didn't keep playing violin
and like keep experimenting.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.810
But the thing was, it wasn't
that I'd played violin,

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.390
it was like seeing you

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.130
and seeing these different
kinds of violin players.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.161
Because not just orchestra
settings like seeing a band...

00:03:28.161 --> 00:03:28.994
- Their all important.

00:03:28.994 --> 00:03:32.060
It's really, it's music,
it's not (voice fades)

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.070
- We really couldn't even
afford violin lessons.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:40.000
My mom suggested that I play
with the choir at my church.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.130
There was no other like
violinist up there.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.720
So then I eventually
developed a sense of ear.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.390
When someone just says anything,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.713
I'm able to like play the same thing.

00:03:51.556 --> 00:03:54.639
(Sudan plays violin)

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.780
There's kind of only
three parts to the song,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.063
which is violin, bass, and drums.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:04.160
Whatever riff I'm playing,
one of those notes

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.934
is gonna be the bass line.

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:08.851
(violin bass riff)

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.880
All the other parts of the
song, that aside from violin,

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.630
are just kind of like simple variations

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.220
to just kind of amplify it.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.558
Definitely like the drums like
if I wanna be like boom clap

00:04:21.558 --> 00:04:25.058
(rhythmic beat on violin)

00:04:26.881 --> 00:04:30.131
(violin riff and bass)

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.480
And the bass on the verse
it was an electric bass.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.010
But on the pre hood, it changes into

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.200
this sub electronic bass.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.450
(electronic bass beat)

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.690
I create all of those
ideas with the violin.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.502
And then I just kinda like
follow the lead of the violin

00:04:52.502 --> 00:04:56.513
with other electronic
instruments in the computer.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:04:59.940
- Lord, we thank you for this food

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:00.950
that we're about to receive

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:02.240
for the nourishment of our bodies,

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.940
in Christ name we pray, Amen.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:04.773
- Amen.

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:08.330
I never really liked my name Brittney,

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.010
and I was like "Momma
I don't like my name."

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.240
She was like "what about Sudan?"

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.240
So I started going by Sudan,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.802
actually my mom, everyone,
just calls me Sudan.

00:05:17.802 --> 00:05:18.635
Mmmm

00:05:18.635 --> 00:05:19.468
- Isn't it good?

00:05:19.468 --> 00:05:20.301
- Mhmm.

00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:26.460
One day I just decided to just
check out the music in Sudan.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.760
And I found all of this really cool music.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.210
And there's like a strong violin culture

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.763
in Sudan specifically, but
also in Africa as a whole.

00:05:35.622 --> 00:05:39.500
("Nyun Taa Jilma" plays)

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.490
I changed it to Sudan Archives

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.750
because of this like ethnomusicologist.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.770
She specifically like studies
like west African fiddling.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.340
And their playing all these
crazy like inharmonics

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.340
and all this stuff with just one string.

00:05:54.201 --> 00:05:58.100
("Nyun Taa Jilma" fades)

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:01.040
There was like never really any other

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.760
black people playing violin.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:06.640
So, when I saw like people
in Africa playing it

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:09.240
that looked like me, that
makes me feel really good

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.610
for some reason.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.316
Maybe I'm not an outcast.

00:06:13.316 --> 00:06:16.149
("Oatmeal" plays)

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.610
Once I changed my name to Sudan Archives,

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:24.680
it just kind of started to click.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.630
It kind of made me want
to keep practicing violin

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.380
because there's so many
different ways you can play it.

00:06:32.632 --> 00:06:33.760
You shouldn't think about it so hard,

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.143
it's just self expression.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:39.080
I was like OK, there's no
excuse to not keep going;

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:42.693
you can literally teach yourself
or make up your own style.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.140
The lyrics are kind of self explanatory,

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.880
but I was feeling a little,
you know, used at the time.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.610
It was like mainly just about
like close relationships,

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.650
but then it's like a bigger picture to it.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.293
The whole moral of it is just
like knowing your self-worth.

00:07:01.293 --> 00:07:04.382
♪ This is my light, don't block the sun ♪

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:07.853
♪ This is my seat, can't you tell ♪

00:07:07.853 --> 00:07:11.253
♪ This is my time, don't waste it up ♪

00:07:11.253 --> 00:07:14.820
♪ This is my land, nont for sale ♪

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:17.320
You can't buy me, you can't sell me,

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:20.510
you can't trade me, you can't
be me (dismissive laugh),

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:21.493
so stop hating.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:28.600
The word "nont", came from
a sign I saw in Ghana.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.060
It was like a big piece of land,

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.020
and I was like "what is that sign?"

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:31.853
And they were like,

00:07:31.853 --> 00:07:32.940
"Oh it's just someone bought the land

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:34.840
"and they're just saying
its nont for sale."

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.520
So I was like "Oh, that's lit!"

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.950
It's like an ode to my
first trip to Africa.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.790
And that was my first plane
ride out the country as well,

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:46.791
so now I have that memory forever.

00:07:46.791 --> 00:07:47.624
(Nont For Sale plays)

00:07:47.624 --> 00:07:50.711
♪ This is my time, don't waste it up ♪

00:07:50.711 --> 00:07:54.109
♪ This is my land, nont for sale ♪

00:07:54.109 --> 00:07:59.109
♪ Eh eh eh eh eh eh eh eh
eh eh eh eh eh eh eh eh eh ♪

00:08:00.341 --> 00:08:02.220
(music fades)

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.300
A lot of people say like
when they see me on stage,

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.770
by myself, triggering
everything, multitasking,

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.423
doing it all, it's
something inspiring to them.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.490
I hope that it can inspire people

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:15.920
to do that in their own
way whatever they're doing.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.713
I wanna create new ideas with my music.

00:08:19.819 --> 00:08:23.069
(violin playing fades)

